{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJW9GTwFTBHQocZEFnfE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A stable set exists exactly when K is even. For K = 2h the only stable set is {(0,h,h), (h,0,h), (h,h,0)}. For odd K there is none. The full proof is in `/work/answer.md`, and I wrote `/work/SUBMIT`.\n\nI confirmed the pattern by exhaustive search for K = 2 to 9 with `bf.py`. It finds one stable set for K = 2, 4, 6, 8 and none for K = 3, 5, 7, 9. I did not run `check.py` separately, and I did not run the search beyond K = 9.\n\nThe proof runs as follows:\n\n1. **Existence for even K.** The symmetric set is internally stable. For external stability, an allocation not dominated by any of the three would need two coordinates ≥ h, and with sum 2h that forces it to be one of the three.\n2. **Pairs in V.** In any stable set, two distinct elements agree in exactly one coordinate and differ in the other two.\n3. **Shared coordinate.** If every pair agrees in the same coordinate, some allocation is dominated by no element, so V is not stable.\n4. **Triangle.** Otherwise V is a triangle {a, b, c}, where a = (p,q,r), b = a + d(e₂−e₃) and c = a + d(e₁−e₃). A fourth element is impossible.\n5. **d > 0.** For each case there is an explicit allocation outside V that nothing in V dominates.\n6. **d < 0.** The same kind of explicit allocation exists unless p = q = h and r = 0. That forces K = 2h and the symmetric set."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13680,"cache_read_input_tokens":18720,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13680,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":532,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f5db019-bb39-4643-89aa-ccb052875abd","uuid":"0a5085